Those random-looking string folders are typical for modern Android installations. Android obfuscates the directory names inside /data/app/ for security, but the actual app package name is hidden right inside them.
Here is how to quickly find your specific app and extract it.
## Step 1: Find the right folder using a quick search
You can search through all those directories at once to find the app you want by its common name (like "whatsapp", "instagram", or your custom app name).
Run this command from your current directory:

find . -name "*.apk" | cut -d/ -f2 | xargs -I {} sh -c 'echo "Folder: {}"; strings {}/base.apk | grep -i "package:" | head -n 1'

This will print each folder name alongside its internal Android package identity so you can easily spot yours.
## Step 2: Alternative quick method (Using ADB)
If you know the package name of your app (e.g., com.example.myapp), you can let Android tell you exactly where it is stored.

   1. Open a new terminal window.
   2. Run this command to find the exact path:
   
   adb shell pm path com.example.myapp
   
   3. It will output something like: package:/data/app/~~2pwJKW7IhkEtDilrJf_ZZQ==/com.example.myapp-.../base.apk

## Step 3: Copy and test with aapt
Once you identify the correct folder from your ls list (for example, ~~2pwJKW7IhkEtDilrJf_ZZQ==), navigate into it to get the APK.

   1. Enter the folder (replace with your target folder):
   
   cd '~~2pwJKW7IhkEtDilrJf_ZZQ=='
   
   2. Look inside: You will see another subfolder. cd into that subfolder, and you will find base.apk.
   3. Copy it out to your main Downloads directory:
   
   cp base.apk ~/Downloads/my_target_app.apk
   
   4. Run your aapt command to start inspecting:
   
   aapt dump badging ~/Downloads/my_target_app.apk
   
   
